\section{Method}\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Problem Formulation}\label{sec:formulation}

Given model $\mathcal{M}$ and input $x$, we sample $N$ outputs $\{y_1, \ldots, y_N\}$ at temperature $T > 0$ via constrained decoding \cite{willard2023guided}, where each $y_i$ is an entity set.
A \textbf{per-sample signal} $c_{\text{ps}}: (x, y_i) \to \mathbb{R}$ scores each output independently; an \textbf{instance-level signal} $c_{\text{il}}: (x, \{y_i\}_{i=1}^N) \to \mathbb{R}$ produces a single score per input (notation in Appendix~\ref{app:notation}).
This distinction is consequential for selection: in our study, 4 of 5 signals are instance-level aggregates that assign identical QE scores to all $N$ candidates. Consensus-proximity (MBR) converts these to per-sample rankings (\S\ref{sec:selection_gap}), but these rankings do not improve selection; only natively per-sample signals can, in principle, exploit oracle headroom.

\subsection{Confidence Signals}\label{sec:signals}

Four are \textit{consistency signals}: \textit{structural} (Soft Jaccard [SJ], entity voting confidence [VC]) and \textit{surface} (exact match [EM], Fleiss' $\kappa$ [FK]); log-probability [LP] is the sole \textit{model-internal}, per-sample signal.

\paragraph{Log-probability (model-internal, per-sample).}
For sample $y_i$ with tokens $t_1, \ldots, t_{|y_i|}$:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:logprob}
c_{\text{lp}}(y_i) = \frac{1}{|y_i|} \sum_{j=1}^{|y_i|} \log p_{\mathcal{M}}(t_j \mid t_{<j}, x)
\end{equation}

\paragraph{Exact match voting (surface, instance-level).}
The proportion of samples producing the most common output:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:em}
c_{\text{em}}(x) = \frac{1}{N} \max_{y} \big|\{i : y_i = y\}\big|
\end{equation}

\paragraph{Fleiss' $\kappa$ (surface, instance-level).}
We adapt \citet{fleiss1971agreement} to entity sets: items are unique (text, type) tuples across all $N$ samples; each sample assigns binary present/absent labels, yielding a $|E_\cup| \times 2$ rating matrix:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:fk}
\kappa = \frac{\bar{P} - \bar{P}_e}{1 - \bar{P}_e}
\end{equation}
where $\bar{P}$ is mean observed and $\bar{P}_e$ expected agreement.
Temperature-based sampling introduces mild negative dependence ($-$0.4\% to $-$4.0\%; Appendix~\ref{app:fk_independence}), which does not inflate FK.
Unlike structural signals, FK relies on exact string matching.

\paragraph{Soft Jaccard (structural, instance-level).}
For samples $y_i, y_j$, we group extractions by type, compute max-weight bipartite matching (Hungarian algorithm, token-level IoU), and define:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:sj}
\text{SJ}(y_i, y_j) = \frac{\sum_{m \in M} \text{sim}(a_m, b_m)}{\sum_{g} \max(|g_i|, |g_j|)}
\end{equation}
where $M$ is the matched set, $\text{sim}(a_m, b_m)$ is the token-level IoU, and $\max(|g_i|, |g_j|)$ penalizes length asymmetry.\footnote{The two normalizer variants produce nearly identical rankings ($|\Delta\rho|{\leq}0.009$; Table~\ref{tab:sj_normalizer}).}
The instance-level QE signal averages over all pairs: $c_{\text{sj}}(x) = \binom{N}{2}^{-1} \sum_{i<j} \text{SJ}(y_i, y_j)$.
For selection, consensus-proximity converts this to per-sample utility $u_{\text{sj}}(y_i) = (N{-}1)^{-1} \sum_{j \neq i} \text{SJ}(y_i, y_j)$, equivalent to MBR decoding \cite{eikema2022sampling}. The same construction applies to FK, EM, and VC.

\paragraph{Entity voting confidence (structural, instance-level).}
For each entity $e$ in the union $E_\cup$ of all samples, we compute frequency $\text{freq}(e) = |\{i : e \in y_i\}| / N$.
The instance-level confidence is the mean frequency:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:voting}
c_{\text{vote}}(x) = \frac{1}{|E_\cup|} \sum_{e \in E_\cup} \text{freq}(e)
\end{equation}
When $E_\cup = \emptyset$, $c_{\mathrm{vc}}(x) = 0$.\footnote{For voted-output \textit{selection}, we include only entities with frequency ${>}N/2$ (standard majority voting).}

\subsection{Evaluation Framework}\label{sec:evaluation}

\paragraph{Quality estimation.}
Spearman $\rho$ between the signal and instance-level F1, and AUROC classifying instances as above/below median F1.
Both quality estimation and selection exclude instances with no gold annotations: $n{=}529$ on SciERC (from 551), $n{=}2{,}756$ on CoNLL (from 3{,}453).\footnote{On CoNLL, full-set ($n{=}3{,}453$) Spearman $\rho$ values are higher than gold-filtered ($n{=}2{,}756$) by $+0.03$--$0.05$ due to the zero/non-zero boundary inflating correlations, paralleling the effect documented for WNUT-17 in Appendix~\ref{app:additional}.}

\paragraph{Sample selection.}
Best-of-$N$ F1: per-sample signals (LP) directly rank candidates; instance-level signals rank via consensus-proximity (MBR utility; see SJ definition above).
Baselines: \textit{oracle} (highest-F1 sample) and \textit{greedy} ($T{=}0$ argmax decoding).

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}

Self-consistency (majority voting over multiple samples) yields large gains on discrete-answer reasoning \citep{wang2023selfconsistency}, and test-time compute scaling has emerged as a complementary axis to model scaling \cite{snell2025scaling, brown2024monkeys}.
But named entity recognition (NER) poses a structurally different challenge: the output is a \textit{set} of entities, two samples may extract overlapping but non-identical sets, and there is no obvious majority to vote on.

Prior work on confidence estimation for LLM-based IE has conflated \textit{quality estimation} (which inputs does the model handle reliably?) with \textit{sample selection} (which of $N$ outputs is best?).
Existing self-consistency methods \cite{wang2023selfconsistency, chen2024usc, taubenfeld2025cisc, kang2025selfcertainty} assume outputs can be grouped by equivalence, an assumption that breaks for entity sets with partial overlap.
Unlike \citet{yao2026consistency}, who demonstrate that self-consistency correlates with task accuracy, we investigate \textit{why} this correlation fails to yield selection improvements, diagnosing degeneracy and compression as the two blocking mechanisms.
Our experiments use fine-tuned models, the dominant deployment paradigm for production NER, and separate quality estimation from best-of-$N$ selection.

A methodological premise structures our analysis: four of five signals (SJ, VC, FK, EM) are instance-level aggregates that by design assign identical scores to all $N$ candidates for a given input; only log-probability (LP) is natively per-sample.
These signals achieve reliable quality estimation ($\rho$ up to 0.38 on the primary test bed, 0.42 on Few-NERD; AUROC up to 0.82; Figure~\ref{fig:overview}), supporting selective prediction and error flagging.
Yet quality estimation does not translate to structural selection, a disparity we term the \textbf{correlation-selection gap} (the disparity between a signal's ability to rank instances reliably and its failure to select the best output within a single instance): structural best-of-$N$ selection matches or degrades greedy decoding ($T{=}0$) despite 12\textendash 14~pp oracle headroom.
The gap has two empirical causes: (a)~\textit{instance degeneracy}, where 14\textendash 47\% of instances yield identical instance-level F1 across all $N$ samples, eliminating selection headroom despite positive within-instance utility\textendash F1 correlation (median $\rho{=}0.52$); and (b)~\textit{weak LP-quality alignment}, where within-instance $\rho(\text{LP}, \text{F1})$ is too low to exploit the remaining headroom (\S\ref{sec:discussion}).
LP selection requires both low degeneracy and adequate alignment: significant at 13.8\% degeneracy with $\rho{\approx}0.5$ ($+1.36$~pp, $p{<}0.001$), sub-significant at 17.8\% with $\rho{\approx}0.24$ ($+0.42$~pp), and absent at 47.4\%.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_overview}
\caption{The correlation-selection gap. \textbf{Left}: consistency signals reliably predict instance-level quality ($\rho$ up to 0.45 on CoNLL, 0.38 on the primary test bed, 0.42 on Few-NERD; AUROC up to 0.82). \textbf{Right}: quality estimation does not translate to selection; all methods approximately match greedy decoding ($T{=}0$) despite 13~pp oracle headroom (\S\ref{sec:discussion}).}
\label{fig:overview}
\end{figure*}

We evaluate five signals from three families (structural, surface, and model-internal) on SciERC \cite{luan2018scierc} as a primary medium-difficulty test bed, with Few-NERD \cite{ding2021fewnerd} (low-degeneracy contrast), CoNLL-2003 \cite{tjongkimsang2003conll} (ceiling-effect control), and WNUT-17 \cite{derczynski2017wnut} (signal-collapse boundary), using two 8B models (Qwen3-8B, LLaMA-3.1-8B) with a 4B ablation (Qwen3-4B).
We vary candidate pool size ($N{\in}\{2,\ldots,32\}$), signal sophistication, and model capacity within the 4B\textendash 8B range; behavior at 70B+ scales remains an open question.
An exploratory extension to relation extraction is reported in Appendix~\ref{app:re_extension}.
Our contributions:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Quality estimation framework.}
    Five signals from three families evaluated on four NER benchmarks, two model families, and a 4B ablation. Structural signals reliably predict quality (VC $\rho{=}0.38$; EM AUROC${=}0.82$), with model-stable rankings supporting selective prediction.

    \item \textbf{Diagnosis of the correlation-selection gap.}
    A three-layer failure: instance-level signals cannot rank candidates by construction; MBR fails because degeneracy (14\textendash 47\%) collapses the candidate pool; LP requires both low degeneracy and adequate alignment ($\rho \gtrsim 0.3$).
    Diagnostic ablations (LoRA rank, free-form decoding, entity-token LP, supervised combination) confirm the bottleneck lies in the signal space, not combination methods.

    \item \textbf{Two necessary conditions for LP selection.}
    The cross-dataset gradient (47\% $\to$ fail, 14\% $\to$ $+1.36$~pp at $p{<}0.001$) establishes degeneracy as one condition; LoRA-rank and temperature manipulations independently confirm alignment as the other. Entity-type-level analysis provides statistical grounding ($\rho{=}{-}0.881$, $p{=}0.004$); LP scales with $N$ in the low-degeneracy regime ($+0.52$~pp, $p{=}0.003$).
\end{enumerate}

